{0 Generating notes: a worked example}

A vault, three templates, and the [dune] rules that turn one into the other.
The JSON namespace the templates read is specified in
{!page-"template-context"}; this page is only about wiring it to a build.

The example is real: it lives in [pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/] and
builds with

{v
dune build @pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/vault-site
v}

{1 The tree}

Authored files, with the vault kept separate from the templates that read it:

{v
pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/
├── dune
├── templates/
│   ├── index.md.jinja
│   ├── tags.md.jinja
│   └── health.md.jinja
└── vault/
    ├── assets/
    │   └── diagram.svg
    ├── notes/
    │   ├── cmarkit.md
    │   └── wikilinks.md
    └── projects/
        ├── mlmdx.md
        └── oyster.md
v}

Building adds three generated notes beside the templates:

{v
├── index.md      ← templates/index.md.jinja
├── tags.md       ← templates/tags.md.jinja
└── health.md     ← templates/health.md.jinja
v}

{1 A note}

Each note carries the frontmatter the templates filter and sort on:

{v
---
title: The Oyster Project
tags: [ocaml, active]
created: 2026-01-15
---
# Oyster

A vault toolchain. Parsing lives in [[notes/cmarkit]], and the link syntax is
described in [[notes/wikilinks]].
v}

{1 The rules}

One rule per generated note. Each depends on the whole vault and on its own
template:

{v
(rule
 (targets index.md)
 (deps
  (source_tree vault)
  templates/index.md.jinja)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   index.md
   (pipe-stdout
    (run %{bin:oyster} context vault)
    (run minijinja-cli --format=json templates/index.md.jinja -)))))

(alias
 (name vault-site)
 (deps index.md tags.md health.md))
v}

Four things in that stanza are load-bearing.

{2 [pipe-stdout], not [pipe-stdin]}

The pipe is named for the output being redirected, so the action that connects
one command's stdout to the next command's stdin is [pipe-stdout]. There is no
[pipe-stdin] action.

{2 [(source_tree vault)]}

A template that queries the whole vault depends on every note in it. The
dependency is therefore coarse on purpose: editing any note rebuilds every
generated page. Fine-grained dependencies would require knowing which notes a
template actually read, which the engine does not report.

It is also correct, which a narrower dependency would not be. Adding a link to
[projects/mlmdx.md] changes the backlink count printed for
[notes/wikilinks.md], and the rule reruns.

{2 A named alias}

[minijinja-cli] is not an OCaml dependency; [(run minijinja-cli ...)] resolves
it from [PATH]. Attaching the generated notes to their own alias rather than to
[@default] keeps a build that never renders from needing the binary at all.

{2 [%{bin:oyster}]}

Resolves the executable from the workspace, so the rule does not depend on
[oyster] being installed.

{1 Templates}

Group every note by directory, and annotate each with its tags and how many
notes point at it:

{v
# {{ vault.note_count }} notes

{% for dir, group in notes | groupby("dir") -%}
## {{ dir if dir else "(root)" }}

{% for n in group -%}
- [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{%- if n.tags %} — {{ n.tags | join(", ") }}{% endif %}
{%- if n.backlink_count %} · {{ n.backlink_count }} backlink(s){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor -%}
v}

which produces

{v
# 4 notes

## notes

- [Cmarkit](notes/cmarkit.md) — ocaml, reference · 2 backlink(s)
- [Wikilinks](notes/wikilinks.md) — reference · 2 backlink(s)

## projects

- [mlmdx](projects/mlmdx.md) — ocaml, paused
- [The Oyster Project](projects/oyster.md) — ocaml, active
v}

Drive an index off the tag map instead, resolving each path through
[notes_by_path]:

{v
{% for tag, paths in tags | items -%}
## {{ tag }} ({{ paths | length }})

{% for p in paths -%}
- [{{ notes_by_path[p].title }}]({{ p }})
{% endfor %}
{% endfor -%}
v}

Or report on the graph rather than listing it:

{v
{{ vault.note_count }} notes · {{ vault.link_count }} links · {{ vault.unresolved_count }} unresolved

## Unresolved links

{% for u in unresolved -%}
- `{{ u.source }}`:{{ u.line }} → `{{ u.target }}` ({{ u.reason }})
{% endfor %}
## Orphans

{% for p in orphans -%}
- `{{ p }}`
{% endfor -%}
v}

{v
4 notes · 6 links · 1 unresolved

## Unresolved links

- `notes/wikilinks.md`:7 → `nonexistent-note` (missing_path)

## Orphans

- `projects/mlmdx.md`
- `projects/oyster.md`
v}

{1 Filtering}

Selecting on a tag is a membership test on the note's [tags], not a
[selectattr] against a list:

{v
{% for n in notes | selectattr("created") | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) %}
{% if "ocaml" in n.tags %}- {{ n.created }} — [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
v}

[selectattr("tags", "in", \["ocaml"\])] silently matches nothing: it tests
whether the whole [tags] list is an element of [\["ocaml"\]]. Sorting on
[created] works directly because the dates are ISO-8601 strings — see
{!page-"template-context".dates}.

{1 Generating into the vault}

Writing a generated note into the vault itself works, and does not create a
cycle: [dune] excludes a rule's own target from the [source_tree] it depends
on, so the generated note does not appear in the context that produced it.

{v
(subdir
 vault
 (rule
  (targets generated.md)
  (deps
   (source_tree .)
   ../templates/index.md.jinja)
  (action
   (with-stdout-to
    generated.md
    (pipe-stdout
     (run %{bin:oyster} context .)
     (run minijinja-cli --format=json ../templates/index.md.jinja -))))))
v}

Keeping generated notes outside the vault is still the simpler arrangement: it
keeps the authored vault the thing an editor and the LSP see, and leaves no
question about which notes are inputs.
